With commit a2142ddcd060d0d2d8a0657527164ca3e257053c on the default 'master'
branch of Boolector I have added an option to disable normalization of adders.
With that disabled '-nadd 0' one can generate interesting CRUX Miters.  The
idea is to take the 'n' input bits (number given as first argument) and then
add them together with bit-vector adders of width 'm = ceil(log2 (n))' in
a random way.   For the first CRUX tree we randomly partition the remaining
not added up inputs in two parts and add those together recursively.  The
same for the second CRUX, but there we permute the inputs first.  Then an
equivalence check is added to the outputs of the two CRUX trees. To generate
a BTOR word level benchmark:

  ./gencruxmitersmt 8 0|boolector -nadd 0 -db

  (can be visualized with 'btorvis')

where n=8 and  seed=0 (the seed) and accordingly AIGs

  ./gencruxmitersmt 8 0|boolector -nadd 0 -daa

  (can be visualized with 'aigvis')

and finally CNF as follows

  ./gencruxmitersmt 8 0|boolector -nadd 0 -dai|aigtocnf
